# Deplaned

Landed. I gather my bags from the overhead. I didn't check anything - just carry-ons. My tee-shirt stretches upwards as I do, to fetch my bags, revealing more of my fat gut than I'd like. But my embarrassment doesn't prevent me from helping those around me fetch their bags - it's the responsibility of the tall to become height for the altitudinally less-fortunate. Sometimes, anyway.

Kind of crazy, making this trip. Can't believe I'm this smitten only a few months into talking. As if it only started now - I've been smitten since mere days talking. She's not really my usual type, visually... but the smile in her photo shines, and there's a spark in her soul that I always strain to seek, but so seldom find. The spark that sparks my own soul.

Conversation has just been so fucking easy, from the very beginning. What is it that compels me to share every moment of what I'm doing, what's going on, to talk for hours and hours on the phone. To share sickeningly cute GIFs from Tenor (GIFs with a hard "g". I don't care that phonetics don't work that way, or that the creator pronounces it "jiff". If "gift" is "guh-ift", then "gif" is "guh-iff"!). Her face and her voice has infected my every waking thought. Pushing out various projects and puzzles and math exercises and programming challenges that were previously there. There are a few left, but not many, and not strongly.

As I enter the tram to the unsecured area, I feel confident... but there is the tiniest little tickle of «Will we jive in person as well as we hope? Is the spark we both feel just an illusion, formed of giddy hopes, born of our long searches for the right Other? Will one of us discover we've been fooling ourselves into believing we just might have found love, when what we really have is the affectionate attention of another?»

But our gazes meet as I step off the tram from the gate, and her presence is as full of sunlight as my heart knew it was. She's gawking at me through the side of her downturned, shy and blusteringly blushing face (not all the red is her rosacia!). In front of her, presented for my appreciation, is a Starbucks caramel macchiato, because of course she remembered my favorite drink. Set beside the pumpkin spice latte that is hers. Hot and ready to quench.

Both of us are staring just a bit, in awe that the beauty we'd manufactured in our heads from a couple of photos and some very brief video calls really did match something real. It exists. She exists. And she's so lovely, and bashful, and... mine. She's for me. And I'm for her. And even my unflinchingly skeptical soul knows: This is how magic starts.

